<p>Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán and opposition leader Péter Magyar each drew large crowds in Budapest on March 15, using Hungary&#x27;s national day to show momentum ahead of the country&#x27;s upcoming election. The rival rallies showed how competitive the campaign has become, with DW describing Magyar as Orbán&#x27;s first serious challenger in years and POLITICO reporting that Tisza is running about 10 percentage points ahead in polls. The two camps offered sharply different visions for Hungary. DW reported that Orbán cast the vote as a fight for sovereignty and resistance to outside pressure, while Magyar presented the election as a chance to change how the state is run. POLITICO said Magyar is recruiting senior business executives for a prospective cabinet, arguing they could help unlock frozen European Union funds and loosen Fidesz&#x27;s hold over institutions such as the judiciary, media, and public companies if Tisza wins next month. For readers across Europe and beyond, the contest matters because Hungary sits at the center of repeated disputes with Brussels over governance and EU funding. That means the election could shape both domestic institutions and Hungary&#x27;s relationship with the bloc.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/RW6JAHO-VeQ12cGkjJKsWUaODM_qKEnGUvmSVZ7MurddTgHuJR8-k88yaNr3Clq_5AiSRJaJkLTvkcpaZ-LcrDGqPDrjpCHPNJfsH9UAku_20CBKiiHP5SOOipzEDt84IND4T8twim4yklYkGbFPzW6Un-b5' alt='Péter Magyar, the Hungarian opposition leader whose Tisza party is challenging Viktor Orbán before the April election.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Staffing philosophy: Tisza says it wants ministers drawn mostly from the business world, looking for &#x27;good managers&#x27; without prior government &#x27;baggage&#x27;.</li><li>Campaign message: Tisza is promising a &#x27;true regime change&#x27; to dismantle what it describes as crony networks across Hungarian public life.</li><li>Show of strength: AllSides described the dueling demonstrations as a public test of each camp&#x27;s ability to mobilize supporters four weeks before the April 12 election.</li><li>Power structure: POLITICO reported that Orbán&#x27;s influence runs through complex Orbán-linked shareholding networks as well as state bodies, which could complicate any transition.</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-03-15-0700/world-orban-and-challenger-magyar-summon-rival-rallies-show-strength-hungarys-april-election'>Orbán and challenger Magyar summon rival rallies in show of strength before Hungary&#x27;s April election</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/hungarian-opposition-picks-top-business-executives-dismantle-orban-rule/'>Hungarian opposition picks top business executives to dismantle Orbán’s rule</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/hungary-s-rival-parties-hold-parallel-rallies-ahead-of-vote/a-76367895'>Hungary&#x27;s rival parties hold parallel rallies ahead of vote</a> - dw.com</li></ul>